---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getFields, getSummary } from '../../lib/data';

const fields = getFields();
const summary = getSummary();
const maxPct = fields.length ? Math.max(...fields.map(f => f.pct)) : 0;
const totalWeight = fields.reduce((acc, f) => acc + f.weight, 0);
---
<Layout title="Per-field breakdown">
  <h1>Per-field breakdown</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The headline "% with any IPTC" hides a lot of detail. Below is the
    population rate of each individual scored field across the {summary.image_count.toLocaleString()}
    images analysed in the latest crawl. Each field also contributes a fixed weight to a site's
    overall score — see <a href="/about/">the methodology</a> for how the score is computed.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Field</th>
        <th class="num">Present</th>
        <th class="num">Total</th>
        <th class="num">Population</th>
        <th class="num">Score weight</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {fields.map(f => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{f.field}</code></td>
          <td class="num">{f.present.toLocaleString()}</td>
          <td class="num">{f.total.toLocaleString()}</td>
          <td class="num">
            {f.pct}%
            <span class="bar" aria-hidden="true">
              <span style={`width:${maxPct ? (100 * f.pct / maxPct) : 0}%`}></span>
            </span>
          </td>
          <td class="num">{f.weight_pct}%</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
    <tfoot>
      <tr>
        <th>Total</th>
        <th class="num"></th>
        <th class="num"></th>
        <th class="num"></th>
        <th class="num">{totalWeight ? '100%' : '—'}</th>
      </tr>
    </tfoot>
  </table>

  <p style="color:var(--iptc-muted);font-size:0.9rem;margin-top:1.5rem">
    Bars are scaled to the most populated field, not to 100%, so differences within the
    long tail stay visible. The score weight column reflects the per-field weights in
    <code>crawler/src/pmd_crawler/scoring.py</code>: Creator, CopyrightNotice and
    CaptionAbstract count for 15% each, with the rest at 5–10%.
  </p>

  <h2>What this tells us</h2>
  <p>
    Even the best-populated field — <code>{fields[0]?.field}</code> at
    <strong>{fields[0]?.pct}%</strong> — appears on fewer than one in ten images we fetched.
    The fields most relevant to licensing (<code>WebStatement</code>, <code>LicensorURL</code>)
    are below 2%. The dataset as a whole tells a consistent story: publishers and CDNs are
    discarding metadata that the photographers and agencies almost certainly embedded
    upstream.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .bar {
    display: inline-block;
    vertical-align: middle;
    margin-left: 0.5rem;
    width: 100px;
    height: 6px;
    background: var(--iptc-bar-bg, #eaecef);
    border-radius: 3px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .bar > span {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    background: var(--iptc-green, #6db33f);
  }
</style>
